tputText": "ls: /private/var/tmp/own023-r3/B-vibe/work/gala/app/node_modules: No such file or directory\n--- npx cache ---\n52f114909794f520\na7af2b55eab49089\n--- home ---\ntotal 0\ndrwxr-xr-x   6 richardosborne  wheel  192 Oct 21 19:54 .\ndrwxr-xr-x  10 richardosborne  wheel  320 Oct 21 19:54 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x   3 richardosborne  wheel   96 Oct 21 19:54 .local\ndrwxr-xr-x   6 richardosborne  wheel  192 Oct 21 19:54 .npm\ndrwx------@  6 richardosborne  wheel  192 Oct 21 19:54 .vibe\ndrwxr-xr-x   4 richardosborne  wheel  128 Oct 21 19:54 tmp\n", "durationMs": 20.963250193744898, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Ran", "message": "ls -la /private/var/tmp/own023-r3/B-vibe/work/gala/app/node_modules 2>&1 | head; echo \"--- npx cache ---\"; ls ~/.npm/_npx 2>/dev/null | head; echo \"--- home ---\"; ls -la ~ 2>/dev/null | head -30", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "2fb811bb-2481-4141-a39c-b8e6dc88a57c", "sessionId": "25505b9f-a8ea-dd62-6b8d-be45f136b4b6", "turnId": "5093e3ac-9153-41c4-bc70-29af6171bbc2", "inputEntryId": null, "createdAt": 1792605690578, "updatedAt": 1792605690625, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "I have enough understanding. Let me quickly check the engine.js to understand `simulate` and the save's role, and the stage/stage.js for the offline concern (does the stage need network? No, it's local 3D). Let me read engine.js partially and stage.js.\n\nActually, the offline concern is about the save sync, not the game itself. The game is entirely client-side (3D, rules). The only network use is: (1) the GameChange collection fetch at startup, (2) the grown-ups' cloud functions. So the game already works offline except for the grown-ups' changes fetch (which has a 4s timeout and falls back). Good.\n\nLet me read engine.js to confirm simulate is pure and to understand the save fields used. And stage/stage.js for the sync. Let me read them.", "summary": []}
{"id": "call_fd3a8a0326d343d29e72dbf0", "sessionId": "25505b9f-a8ea-dd62-6b8d-be45f136b4b6", "turnId": "5093e3ac-9153-41c4-bc70-29af6171bbc2", "inputEntryId": null, "createdAt": 1792605690625, "updatedAt": 1792605690639, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "read_file", "detail": {"toolName": "read_file", "display": {"summary": "Reading app/scripts/rules/engine.js (limit 120 lines)", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Reading", "message": "app/scripts/rules/engine.js (limit 120 lines)", "settledVerb": "Read", "settledMessage": "app/scripts/rules/engine.js (limit 120 lines)", "statusText": "Reading file"}, "kind": "file_read", "input": {"filePath": "/private/var/tmp/own023-r3/B-vibe/work/gala/app/scripts/rules/engine.js", "offset": null, "limit": 120}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"filePath": "/private/var/tmp/own023-r3/B-vibe/work/gala/app/scripts/rules/engine.js", "content": "        1→// GENERATED from gala-rules/src/engine.js by tools/sync-rules.mjs — do not edit here, edit gala-rules and run it again.\n        2→// The gala day as a pure function of the plan and the school: simulate() returns every verdict, every dancer's schedule,\n        3→// the points and a trace of { t, who, step, verdict, say }. No DOM, no three.js, no words: the sheet turns `say` into\n        4→// sentences. This is the engine the port would package; tests/engine.test.mjs runs it in Node.\n        5→const {\n        6→  RULES, CLS, NUMBERS, numDur, OTHER_ITEMS, itemKind, STYLES, MOVES, OUTFITS, HAIRS, SHOES, ACCS, PIECES, LOGE,\n        7→  doorTiles, SNACKS, BACKDROP_THEMES, PARKING, seasonRules, hm,\n        8→} = use('rules/data');\n        9→\n       10→const clamp = (v, a, b) => Math.max(a, Math.min(b, v));\n       11→const rngFor = (seed) => { let a = seed >>> 0; return () => { a = (a + 0x6D2B79F5) >>> 0; let x = a; x = Math.imul(x ^ (x >>> 15), x | 1); x ^= x + Math.imul(x ^ (x >>> 7), x | 61); return ((x ^ (x >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296; }; };\n       12→const dancerId = (c, i) => `${c}-${i}`;\n       13→const mean = (a) => (a.length ? a.reduce((s, x) => s + x, 0) / a.length : 0);\n       14→\n       15→/* ---------------- what she owns ---------------- */\n       16→// kind: 'outfit' | 'hair' | 'shoes' | 'acc' | 'piece' | 'snack' | 'up' (hall and car park) | 'food'\n       17→const CATALOG = { outfit: OUTFITS, hair: HAIRS, shoes: SHOES, acc: ACCS, piece: PIECES, snack: SNACKS };\n       18→function isOwned(school, kind, id) {\n       19→  if (kind === 'up') return !!school.upgrades[id];\n       20→  const item = CATALOG[kind] ? CATALOG[kind][id] : null;\n       21→  if (kind === 'food') return !!school.owned[`food:${id}`];\n       22→  return !!item && (!item.price || !!school.owned[`${kind}:${id}`]);\n       23→}\n       24→const stageOf = (school) => (school.upgrades.scene ? RULES.stageBig : RULES.stage);\n       25→const seatsOf = (school) => 112 + 14 * (school.upgrades.sieges || 0);\n       26→\n       27→/* ---------------- the dressing room ---------------- */\n       28→function footprint(f) { const p = PIECES[f.id]; return f.rot % 2 ? { w: p.d, d: p.w } : { w: p.w, d: p.d }; }\n       29→// a piece that hangs on a wall must have its back against one: turning it changes which side is its back\n       30→function againstWall(f, cols) {\n       31→  const fp = footprint(f);\n       32→  switch (((f.rot % 4) + 4) % 4) {\n       33→    case 0: return f.r === 0;\n       34→    case 1: return f.c + fp.w === cols;\n       35→    case 2: return f.r + fp.d === LOGE.rows;\n       36→    default: return f.c === 0;\n       37→  }\n       38→}\n       39→function fits(f, list, cols, ignore) {\n       40→  const fp = footprint(f);\n       41→  if (f.c < 0 || f.r < 0 || f.c + fp.w > cols || f.r + fp.d > LOGE.rows) return false;\n       42→  if (PIECES[f.id].flat) return true;\n       43→  for (const [c, r] of doorTiles(cols)) if (c >= f.c && c < f.c + fp.w && r >= f.r && r < f.r + fp.d) return false;\n       44→  for (const o of list) {\n       45→    if (o === ignore || PIECES[o.id].flat) continue;\n       46→    const q = footprint(o);\n       47→    if (f.c < o.c + q.w && o.c < f.c + fp.w && f.r < o.r + q.d && o.r < f.r + fp.d) return false;\n       48→  }\n       49→  return true;\n       50→}\n       51→function analyseLoge(school, totalDancers) {\n       52→  const cols = school.logeCols, fx = { postes: 0, rail: 0, seats: 0, calm: 0, water: 0, fruit: 0, fan: 0, shoes: 0, screen: 0, sieste: 0, doudous: 0, barre: 0, clock: 0, music: 0 };\n       53→  const invalid = []; let covered = 0;\n       54→  for (const f of school.furniture) {\n       55→    const p = PIECES[f.id]; if (!p) continue;\n       56→    const fp = footprint(f); if (!p.flat) covered += fp.w * fp.d;\n       57→    if (p.wall && !againstWall(f, cols)) { invalid.push(f); continue; }\n       58→    for (const k of Object.keys(p.fx)) fx[k] += p.fx[k];\n       59→  }\n       60→  const tiles = cols * LOGE.rows;\n       61→  return {\n       62→    ...fx, postesRaw: fx.postes, postes: Math.max(1, fx.postes), calmRaw: fx.calm, calm: Math.min(30, fx.calm),\n       63→    railCap: fx.rail * RULES.railCap, covered, tiles, clutter: covered / tiles > 0.5, invalid, totalDancers,\n       64→    seatRatio: Math.min(1, fx.seats / Math.max(1, totalDancers)),\n       65→  };\n       66→}\n       67→// minutes for a class to change from one number's costume to the next\n       68→function changeTime(prev, next, loge) {\n       69→  const lines = [['outfit', OUTFITS[next.outfit].min, next.outfit]];\n       70→  if (prev.shoes !== next.shoes) lines.push(['shoes', loge.shoes ? 1 : 2]);\n       71→  if (prev.acc !== next.acc && next.acc !== 'aucun') lines.push(['acc', 1]);\n       72→  if (loge.railCap < loge.totalDancers) lines.push(['rails', 3]);\n       73→  if (loge.screen) lines.push(['screen', -1]);\n       74→  if (loge.clutter) lines.push(['clutter', 2]);\n       75→  const sum = lines.reduce((s, l) => s + l[1], 0);\n       76→  return { min: Math.max(2, sum), sum, lines };\n       77→}\n       78→\n       79→/* ---------------- a dance: the phrase, the spacing, the music ---------------- */\n       80→// a dance: named blocks of steps, and the chain that says in which order the class dances them (a block can come back as\n       81→// often as she likes, like a component placed many times). The engine reads the chain flat: one long run of steps, danced once.\n       82→function danceOf(np) {\n       83→  const byId = Object.fromEntries(np.blocks.map((b) => [b.id, b])), moves = [], marks = [], times = {};\n       84→  np.chain.forEach((bid, j) => { const b = byId[bid]; if (!b) return; marks.push({ j, block: bid, k0: moves.length, k1: moves.length + b.moves.length }); moves.push(...b.moves); times[bid] = (times[bid] || 0) + 1; });\n       85→  return { A: np.A, B: np.B, costume: np.costume, moves, repeats: 1, marks, chainLen: marks.length, mostRepeated: Math.max(0, ...Object.values(times)), blocksUsed: Object.keys(times).length };\n       86→}\n       87→// cyclic: the last step leads back into the first (true for a block shown on its own, which may repeat; false for a whole dance)\n       88→function routineInfo(moves, style, cyclic = true) {\n       89→  const M = moves.map((id) => MOVES[id]);\n       90→  const phrase = M.reduce((s, m) => s + m.c, 0);\n       91→  const links = (cyclic ? M : M.slice(0, -1)).map((m, k) => {\n       92→    const n = M[(k + 1) % M.length], gap = Math.abs(m.b - n.a), off = n.st !== '*' && !n.st.split(' ').includes(style);\n       93→    let score = gap === 0 ? 1 : gap === 1 ? 0.6 : 0; if (off) score *= 0.5;\n       94→    return { k, from: moves[k], to: moves[(k + 1) % M.length], gap, off, score };\n       95→  });\n       96→  let dx = 0, dz = 0; const offsets = M.map((m) => { dx += m.dx || 0; dz += m.dz || 0; return { dx, dz }; });\n       97→  const hardPairs = M.length > 1 ? links.filter((l) => MOVES[l.from].d >= 2 && MOVES[l.to].d >= 2).length : 0;\n       98→  return {\n       99→    phrase, links, avgHarmony: links.length ? mean(links.map((l) => l.score)) : 1, avgDiff: phrase ? M.reduce((s, m) => s + m.d * m.c, 0) / phrase : 0,\n      100→    offsets, net: { dx, dz }, swaps: M.filter((m) => m.swap).length, distinct: new Set(moves.filter((id) => !MOVES[id].swap)).size,\n      101→    hardPairs, starts: M.reduce((a, m, k) => (a.push(k ? a[k - 1] + M[k - 1].c : 0), a), []),\n      102→  };\n      103→}\n      104→// where every dancer is after each move of each repetition; the spacing judged there\n      105→function judgeSpacing(np, cls, stage, info) {\n      106→  const n = cls.n, A = np.A, B = info.swaps ? (np.B || np.A) : null, span = cls.span, out = { collide: [], hidden: [], offstage: [], bump: [], moved: 0 };\n      107→  const statics = B ? [['A', A], ['B', B]] : [['A', A]];\n      108→  for (const [tag, P] of statics) for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n      109→    const d = Math.hypot(P[i].x - P[j].x, P[i].z - P[j].z);\n      110→    if (d < span - 1e-9) out.collide.push({ i, j, d, tag });\n      111→    else if (Math.abs(P[i].x - P[j].x) < RULES.hideDx - 1e-9) out.hidden.push(P[i].z > P[j].z ? { b: i, f: j, tag, dx: Math.abs(P[i].x - P[j].x) } : { b: j, f: i, tag, dx: Math.abs(P[i].x - P[j].x) });\n      112→  }\n      113→  if (B) out.moved = Math.max(...A.map((p, i) => Math.hypot(p.x - B[i].x, p.z - B[i].z)));\n      114→  const off = (p) => p.x > stage.halfW + 1e-9 || p.x < -stage.halfW - 1e-9 || p.z < -1e-9 || p.z > stage.depth + 1e-9;\n      115→  const seenOff = new Set(), seenBump = new Set(), R = np.repeats, mv = np.moves;\n      116→  let F = A, ox = 0, oz = 0;\n      117→  const check = (P, r, k) => P.forEach((p, i) => { if (!seenOff.has(i) && off({ x: p.x + ox, z: p.z + oz })) { seenOff.add(i); out.offstage.push({ i, r, k, x: p.x + ox, z: p.z + oz, x0: p.x, z0: p.z }); } });\n      118→  check(F, 0, -1);\n      119→  for (let r = 0; r < R && r < 60; r++) for (let k = 0; k < mv.length; k++) {\n      120→    const m = MOVES[mv[k]];", "numLines": 120, "startLine": 1, "requestedOffset": null, "requestedLimit": 120, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": true}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 1.7039170488715172, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "120 lines from app/scripts/rules/engine.js", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": "(truncated)"}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "call_2403e3817e4147a4bf356a6f", "sessionId": "25505b9f-a8ea-dd62-6b8d-be45f136b4b6", "turnId": "5093e3ac-9153-41c4-bc70-29af6171bbc2", "inputEntryId": null, "createdAt": 1792605690627, "updatedAt": 1792605690640, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "read_file", "detail": {"toolName": "read_file", "display": {"summary": "Reading app/scripts/stage/stage.js", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Reading", "message": "app/scripts/stage/stage.js", "settledVerb": "Read", "settledMessage": "app/scripts/stage/stage.js", "statusText": "Reading file"}, "kind": "file_read", "input": {"filePath": "/private/var/tmp/own023-r3/B-vibe/work/gala/app/scripts/stage/stage.js", "offset": null, "limit": 2000}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"filePath": "/private/var/tmp/own023-r3/B-vibe/work/gala/app/scripts/stage/stage.js", "content": "        1→// The 3D view and the music, put together: what reference/src/main.js did (the frame loop, the pointer and keys on\n        2→// the canvas) and the half of ui.js that moved the world (refreshWorld, camCmd, setTab's camera cuts, tapAt,\n        3→// hoverAt, placeFloat, resize, beginRun/stopRun). The graph owns the state: it calls sync() with the save and the\n        4→// screen state whenever either changes, and hears back through env.emit(kind, data). Nothing here draws a page\n        5→// element; the only element it owns is three.js's own canvas.\n        6→const World = use('stage/world'), People = use('stage/people'), Play = use('stage/play'), Music = use('stage/music');\n        7→const { CLS, NUMBERS, PIECES, LOGE, seasonRules } = use('rules/data');\n        8→const { stageOf, fits, againstWall, footprint, clamp } = use('rules/engine');\n        9→\n       10→function create(env) {\n       11→  const window = env.window, document = env.document, SIZE = env.size;\n       12→  const G = { S: env.save, lang: env.save.lang || 'fr', screen: 'prep', tab: 'order', run: null, speed: 1, follow: true, pickedRoom: null, armed: null, selected: null, pickNum: 'G1', editB: false, pickDancer: null, ruler: null, realT: 0, frames: 0 };\n       13→  const base = { T: env.T, window, document, size: SIZE, G, save: () => {} };\n       14→  const W = World.create({ ...base, mods: {} });\n       15→  const P = People.create({ ...base, mods: { world: W } });\n       16→  const PL = Play.create({ ...base, mods: { world: W, people: P } });\n       17→  const MU = Music.create({ ...base, mods: {} });\n       18→  const emit = (kind, data) => env.emit(kind, data);\n       19→  const canvas = W.canvas;\n       20→  let schoolName = env.schoolName || '';\n       21→\n       22→  /* ---------------- the world follows the save ---------------- */\n       23→  function refreshWorld() {\n       24→    const sc = G.S.school, up = sc.upgrades;\n       25→    W.buildLoge(); W.showFurniture(); W.buildSeats(up.sieges || 0, !!up.confort); W.showBuvette(up.buvette); W.buildLights(up);\n       26→    W.buildParking(G.S.plan.parking.width, up.rangee ? 2 : 1); W.showBackdrop(G.S.plan.backdrop); W.drawStageTape(stageOf(sc)); W.drawSign(schoolName); W.setStageFloor('tapis');\n       27→    if (!G.run) PL.idleScene();\n       28→  }\n       29→  const camState = () => ({ mode: W.view.mode, high: W.view.high, wallMode: W.wallMode });\n       30→  const sendCam = () => emit('cam', camState());\n       31→  // the reference compared the selected piece by identity; the graph sends a copy, so find the piece it means\n       32→  const findPiece = (f) => (f ? G.S.school.furniture.find((x) => x.id === f.id && x.c === f.c && x.r === f.r && x.rot === f.rot) || null : null);\n       33→\n       34→  let worldKey = '', classKey = '', lastTab = null, lastRoom = null, lastNum = null;\n       35→  function sync(save, ui, name) {\n       36→    G.S = save; G.lang = save.lang || 'fr'; schoolName = name || schoolName;\n       37→    G.screen = ui.screen || 'prep'; G.pickNum = ui.pickNum || G.pickNum; G.editB = !!ui.editB; G.pickDancer = ui.pickDancer ?? null;\n       38→    G.armed = ui.armed || null; G.selected = findPiece(ui.selected);\n       39→    if (G.run) { G.speed = ui.speed || 1; G.follow = ui.follow !== false; }\n       40→    if (!G.armed) W.setGhost(null);\n       41→    const ck = JSON.stringify([save.season, seasonRules(save.season).classes]);\n       42→    if (ck !== classKey) { classKey = ck; PL.buildDancers(); }\n       43→    const wk = JSON.stringify([save.school.logeCols, save.school.furniture, save.school.upgrades, save.plan.parking.width, save.plan.backdrop, schoolName, G.selected]);\n       44→    if (wk !== worldKey) { worldKey = wk; refreshWorld(); }\n       45→    // a tab sends the camera where its work is (setTab); a picked room is looked at from close up (pickRoom)\n       46→    const tab = ui.tab || 'order';\n       47→    if (tab !== lastTab && G.screen === 'prep') {\n       48→      lastTab = tab; G.tab = tab;\n       49→      if (tab === 'formation') W.cutTo('stage');\n       50→      else if (tab === 'costumes') W.cutTo('podium');\n       51→      else if (tab === 'parking') W.cutTo('park');\n       52→      else if (tab !== 'build' && ['podium', 'park', 'stage'].includes(W.view.mode)) W.cutTo('school');\n       53→      sendCam();\n       54→    }\n       55→    G.tab = tab;\n       56→    const room = ui.pickedRoom || null;\n       57→    if (room !== lastRoom) { lastRoom = room; G.pickedRoom = room; W.cutTo(room ? 'room' : 'school'); sendCam(); }\n       58→    if (G.screen === 'prep' && G.pickNum !== lastNum && tab === 'formation') { lastNum = G.pickNum; W.cutTo('stage'); }\n       59→    MU.setSound(save.sound !== false);\n       60→    if (!G.run) { for (const d of PL.dancers) if (d.wearing) PL.dress(d, d.wearing); PL.idle(); PL.idleScene(); }\n       61→    W.showFurniture();\n       62→  }\n       63→\n       64→  /* ---------------- the camera bar ---------------- */\n       65→  function camCmd(c) {\n       66→    const v = W.view;\n       67→    if (c === 'rotL' || c === 'rotR') { v.rot = (v.rot + (c === 'rotL' ? 3 : 1)) % 4; if (['stage', 'podium', 'park'].includes(v.mode)) v.mode = G.pickedRoom ? 'room' : 'school'; W.cutTo(); }\n       68→    if (c === 'high') { v.high = !v.high; if (['stage', 'podium'].includes(v.mode)) v.mode = 'school'; W.cutTo(); }\n       69→    if (c === 'school') { if (G.pickedRoom) emit('ui', { pickedRoom: null }); G.pickedRoom = null; lastRoom = null; W.cutTo('school'); }\n       70→    if (c === 'stage') W.cutTo(v.mode === 'stage' ? 'school' : 'stage');\n       71→    if (c === 'park') { if (G.pickedRoom) emit('ui', { pickedRoom: null }); G.pickedRoom = null; lastRoom = null; W.cutTo(v.mode === 'park' ? 'school' : 'park'); }\n       72→    if (c === 'walls') W.setWallMode((W.wallMode + 1) % 3);\n       73→    if (c === 'in') W.zoomBy(1.25); if (c === 'out') W.zoomBy(0.8);\n       74→    if (G.run) G.run.auto = 'manual';\n       75→    sendCam();\n       76→  }\n       77→\n       78→  /* ---------------- placing furniture in the dressing room ---------------- */\n       79→  function showGhost(f) { const cols = G.S.school.logeCols, ok = fits(f, G.S.school.furniture, cols); W.setGhost(f, !ok ? 'bad' : PIECES[f.id].wall && !againstWall(f, cols) ? 'warn' : 'ok'); }\n       80→  function hoverAt(x, y) {\n       81→    if (!G.armed || G.pickedRoom !== 'dr' || G.run) return;\n       82→    const tl = W.tileAt(W.groundAt(x, y)); if (!tl) { W.setGhost(null); return; }\n       83→    const cols = G.S.school.logeCols, fp = footprint({ id: G.armed.id, rot: G.armed.rot });\n       84→    const f = { id: G.armed.id, rot: G.armed.rot, c: clamp(tl.c - Math.floor((fp.w - 1) / 2), 0, cols - fp.w), r: clamp(tl.r - Math.floor((fp.d - 1) / 2), 0, LOGE.rows - fp.d) };\n       85→    const g = W.ghost.f; if (!g || g.c !== f.c || g.r !== f.r || g.rot !== f.rot || g.id !== f.id) showGhost(f);\n       86→  }\n       87→  function tapAt(x, y) {\n       88→    if (G.run) return;\n       89→    if (G.tab === 'build' && G.pickedRoom === 'dr') {\n       90→      if (G.armed) { hoverAt(x, y); const g = W.ghost.f; if (g && fits(g, G.S.school.furniture, G.S.school.logeCols)) { emit('place', { id: g.id, c: g.c, r: g.r, rot: g.rot }); W.setGhost(null); } return; }\n       91→      const f = W.pieceAt(x, y); if (f) { emit('ui', { selected: { ...f } }); return; }\n       92→      if (G.selected) { emit('ui', { selected: null }); return; }\n       93→    }\n       94→    const g = W.groundAt(x, y); if (!g) return; const r = W.roomAt(g.x, g.z);\n       95→    const inside = g.x > -15 && g.x < W.ROOMS.dr.x1 && g.z > -9 && g.z < 7;\n       96→    if (!inside) { if (G.pickedRoom) emit('ui', { pickedRoom: null }); return; }\n       97→    const room = r === 'wing' || r === 'stage' ? 'hall' : r; if (room !== G.pickedRoom) emit('ui', { pickedRoom: room });\n       98→  }\n       99→  function rotateArmed() { if (!G.armed) return; emit('ui', { armed: { ...G.armed, rot: (G.armed.rot + 1) % 4 } }); }\n      100→  // the little toolbar over a selected piece: the graph draws it, this says where\n      101→  let floatKey = '';\n      102→  function placeFloat() {\n      103→    const f = G.selected; let at = { show: false, x: 0, y: 0 };\n      104→    if (f && !G.run && G.tab === 'build' && G.screen === 'prep') {\n      105→      const fp = footprint(f), p = W.project(W.ROOMS.dr.x0 + f.c + fp.w / 2, 1.6, W.ROOMS.dr.z0 + f.r + fp.d / 2);\n      106→      if (!p.behind) at = { show: true, x: Math.round(p.x), y: Math.round(p.y) };\n      107→    }\n      108→    const k = `${at.show}|${at.x}|${at.y}`; if (k !== floatKey) { floatKey = k; emit('float', at); }\n      109→  }\n      110→\n      111→  /* ---------------- the run ---------------- */\n      112→  let liveK = -1;\n      113→  const liveOf = (run) => ({ mode: run.mode, t: run.t, done: run.done, tryId: run.tryId || null, feed: run.feed.map((f) => ({ t: f.t, say: f.text })) });\n      114→  // play.js turns each trace line into words through onSay; the graph owns the words, so it gets the line itself\n      115→  PL.onSay((say) => say, (say) => emit('say', say));\n      116→  function beginRun(mode, tryId) {\n      117→    G.pickedRoom = null; G.armed = null; W.setGhost(null); G.selected = null; W.showFurniture();\n      118→    const run = PL.startRun(mode, tryId); if (!run) return null;\n      119→    MU.wake(); G.speed = 1;\n      120→    run.onDone = () => emit('done', liveOf(run));\n      121→    if (mode !== 'try' && G.follow) W.cutTo('school');\n      122→    sendCam(); emit('live', liveOf(run));\n      123→    return run.res;\n      124→  }\n      125→  function stopRun() { G.run = null; PL.idle(); PL.idleScene(); if (G.tab === 'formation') W.cutTo('stage'); sendCam(); }\n      126→  const skipNumber = () => { PL.skipNumber(); if (G.run) emit('live', liveOf(G.run)); };\n      127→  const skipToEnd = () => { if (G.run) PL.fastForward(G.run.endT - G.run.t + 1); if (G.run) emit('live', liveOf(G.run)); };\n      128→\n      129→  /* ---------------- the pointer and the keys on the 3D view (main.js) ---------------- */\n      130→  const ptrs = new Map(); let pinch0 = 0, zoom0 = 1;\n      131→  canvas.style.touchAction = 'none';\n      132→  canvas.addEventListener('contextmenu', (e) => { e.preventDefault(); if (G.armed) rotateArmed(); });\n      133→  canvas.addEventListener('pointerdown', (e) => { ptrs.set(e.pointerId, { x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY, x0: e.clientX, y0: e.clientY, t0: window.performance.now(), button: e.button, type: e.pointerType }); canvas.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId); if (ptrs.size === 2) { const [a, b] = [...ptrs.values()]; pinch0 = Math.hypot(a.x - b.x, a.y - b.y); zoom0 = W.view.zoom; } });\n      134→  canvas.addEventListener('pointermove', (e) => {\n      135→    const p = ptrs.get(e.pointerId); if (!p) { hoverAt(e.clientX, e.clientY); return; }\n      136→    const dx = e.clientX - p.x, dy = e.clientY - p.y; p.x = e.clientX; p.y = e.clientY;\n      137→    if (Math.hypot(p.x - p.x0, p.y - p.y0) < 5 && ptrs.size === 1) return;\n      138→    if (ptrs.size === 1) W.panBy(dx, dy); else if (ptrs.size === 2) { const [a, b] = [...ptrs.values()]; W.view.zoom = zoom0; W.zoomBy(Math.max(1, Math.hypot(a.x - b.x, a.y - b.y)) / Math.max(1, pinch0)); }\n      139→  });\n      140→  const endPtr = (e) => { const p = ptrs.get(e.pointerId); ptrs.delete(e.pointerId); if (!p || e.type === 'pointercancel' || ptrs.size) return; if (Math.hypot(e.clientX - p.x0, e.clientY - p.y0) > 8 || window.performance.now() - p.t0 > 450 || (p.type === 'mouse' && p.button !== 0)) return; tapAt(e.clientX, e.clientY); };\n      141→  canvas.addEventListener('pointerup', endPtr); canvas.addEventListener('pointercancel', endPtr);\n      142→  canvas.addEventListener('wheel', (e) => { e.preventDefault(); const unit = e.deltaMode === 1 ? 16 : 1; W.zoomBy(Math.exp(-e.deltaY * unit * (e.ctrlKey ? 0.01 : 0.002))); }, { passive: false });\n      143→  const onKey = (e) => {\n      144→    if (e.target && e.target.matches && e.target.matches('input, textarea')) return;\n      145→    if (e.key === 'ArrowLeft' || e.key === 'ArrowRight') { W.panBy(e.key === 'ArrowLeft' ? 60 : -60, 0); e.preventDefault(); }\n      146→    if (e.key === 'ArrowUp' || e.key === 'ArrowDown') { W.panBy(0, e.key === 'ArrowUp' ? 60 : -60); e.preventDefault(); }\n      147→    if (e.key === '+' || e.key === '=') W.zoomBy(1.25); if (e.key === '-') W.zoomBy(0.8);\n      148→    if (e.key === 'q' || e.key === '[') camCmd('rotL'); if (e.key === 'e' || e.key === ']') camCmd('rotR');\n      149→    if (e.key === 'r' || e.key === 'R') { if (G.armed) rotateArmed(); else if (G.selected) emit('key', 'rotateSelected'); }\n      150→    if (e.key === 'Escape') { if (G.armed) emit('key', 'cancelArmed'); else if (G.pickedRoom && G.tab !== 'build') emit('ui', { pickedRoom: null }); }\n      151→  };\n      152→  window.addEventListener('keydown', onKey);\n      153→\n      154→  /* ---------------- the frame loop ---------------- */\n      155→  // the sheet covers the right 400 px on a wide window, the bottom on a narrow one: the camera frames what is left\n      156→  function resize() {\n      157→    const w = SIZE.w, h = SIZE.h; if (w < 50 || h < 50) return;\n      158→    const side = w > 760, under = side ? 0 : Math.min(h * 0.6, h * 0.56);\n      159→    W.resizeRenderer(side ? 400 : 0, under, Math.min(h * 0.4, 100));\n      160→  }\n      161→  let last = window.performance.now(), raf = 0, stopped = false;\n      162→  function frame(now) {\n      163→    if (stopped) return;\n      164→    raf = window.requestAnimationFrame(frame); G.frames++;\n      165→    const dt = Math.min(0.05, (now - last) / 1000); last = now; G.realT += dt;\n      166→    PL.stepRun(dt); MU.tick();\n      167→    if (G.run) {\n      168→      PL.stepScene(dt);\n      169→      if (Math.floor(G.realT * 4) !== liveK && !G.run.done) { liveK = Math.floor(G.realT * 4); emit('live', liveOf(G.run)); }\n      170→    } else W.stepLights(dt, false, false);\n      171→    W.curtain.open += (W.curtain.target - W.curtain.open) * Math.min(1, dt * 3);\n      172→    W.curtain.halves.forEach((g) => { g.scale.x = 1 - W.curtain.open * 0.86; });\n      173→    PL.updateDancers(dt); W.spinFans(dt);\n      174→    W.placeCamera(dt); placeFloat();\n      175→    W.renderer.render(W.scene, W.camera);\n      176→  }\n      177→  raf = window.requestAnimationFrame(frame);\n      178→  if (document.fonts && document.fonts.ready) document.fonts.ready.then(() => W.drawSign(schoolName));\n      179→\n      180→  function destroy() { stopped = true; window.cancelAnimationFrame(raf); window.removeEventListener('keydown', onKey); W.renderer.dispose(); }\n      181→\n      182→  sync(env.save, env.ui || {}, schoolName); PL.buildDancers(); refreshWorld(); PL.idle(); PL.idleScene(); resize(); sendCam();\n      183→  return {\n      184→    canvas, sync, camCmd, resize, beginRun, stopRun, skipNumber, skipToEnd, rotateArmed, destroy,\n      185→    setSpeed: (s) => { G.speed = s; }, setFollow: (f) => { G.follow = f; if (G.run) G.run.auto = ''; },\n      186→    wake: () => MU.wake(), music: () => MU.musicState(), G, W, PL,\n      187→  };\n      188→}\n      189→exports.create = create;", "numLines": 189, "startLine": 1, "requestedOffset": null, "requestedLimit": 2000, "totalLines": 189, "wasTruncated": false}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 2.8217078652232885, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "189 lines from app/scripts/stage/stage.js", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "b4aa540b-d3e3-43b5-8af7-006c6e3fb8e7", "sessionId": "25505b9f-a8ea-dd62-6b8d-be45f136b4b6", "turnId": "5093e3ac-9153-41c4-bc70-29af6171bbc2", "inputEntryId": null, "createdAt": 1792605758458, "updatedAt": 1792605759598, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "I now have a thorough understanding. Let me also quickly check the gala-rules engine test and the music test to know the test setup, and check the `net.noodl.user.*` node semantics. Since I can't access node-spec, I'll rely on the observed usage: `User` node outputs `authenticated`, `roles`, `username`, and has a `fetch` input. `SignUp` has `username`, `password`, `signup` inputs and `done`/`failure`/`error` outputs. `LogIn` has `username`, `password`, `login` inputs and `done`/`failure`/`error`. `LogOut` has `login` input (odd, but that's what the connection shows: `gp_logout.click -> gp_logout1.login`).\n\nNow let me think about the plan in detail.\n\n## Understanding the game today\n\n**What it is:** \"Le Gala de danse\" — a French/English dance-school management game for children. The player is the school's director: they plan the evening's programme (order of numbers), choreograph each number (blocks of dance moves, chained), place dancers in formations, choose costumes/hair/shoes/accessories, decorate the dressing room, set up the car park and shop, and then run a rehearsal or the gala night, which is simulated and scored. Progress is a \"save\" (season, plan, school, notebook).\n\n**How it's put together:**\n- A NodeGX app (folder of JSON). `app/nodegx.project.json` (name \"Le Gala de danse\", id, design tokens, cloudservices endpoint `http://127.0.0.1:8585`, appId `backend_mvi4i1s8eudt8`).\n- Components under `app/components/`: `App` (Router, start page `/Pages/Game`, routes `/Pages/Game`, `/Pages/Grownups`), `Pages/Game`, `Pages/Grownups`, `Logic/Start Up`, `Game/*` (Dispatcher, Stage 3D, Top Bar, Sheet, Results, Live, Toast, Camera Bar, Furniture Toolbar, Season Letter), `Tabs/*` (Order, Formation, Gouter, Costumes, Build, Parking, Shop, Notebook), `UI/*` (many small visual components), `Grownups/*` (Words, New Step, New Costume, Row Editor), and `__cloud__/*` (saveChange, removeChange, claimGrownup).\n- Scripts under `app/scripts/`: `rules/` (data, plan, engine, tunes — generated from `gala-rules/src` by `tools/sync-rules.mjs`), `game/` (save, dispatch, actions, text), `view/` (common, frame, prep, formation, run, grownups), `settings/` (check, apply — check generated from `tools/check.src.js` by `tools/sync-check.mjs`), `stage/` (stage, world, people, play, music — generated from a `reference/src` that is no longer in the folder, by `tools/sync-stage.mjs`).\n- `gala-rules/` is the pure rules package (ES modules) with tests (`node --test tests/*.test.mjs`).\n- `tools/` holds the sync/check scripts and the words data.\n\n**State:** Two Global Stores:\n- `gala` (persist: true, storageKey `gala-game-v2`) — holds `save`.\n- `ui` (persist: false) — holds `ui`, `res`, `live`, `cam`, `float`, `toast`.\n\n**The save:** `game/save.js` defines `fresh(lang)`, `mend(input)`, `nextSeason(save)`, `fromRound1(o)`. Shape: `{ v: 2, lang, season, galaDone, seenIntro, plan, school, notebook, sound, seed }`. `plan` from `rules/plan.js` (`defaultPlan`), `school` from `defaultSchool`.\n\n**The loop:** Every button sends an event on channel `gala-act` with `act`/`arg`. `Game/Dispatcher`'s `dp_fn` calls `game/dispatch.js` `dispatch(act, arg, save, ui, w, stage)`, which returns `{ save, ui, toast }`; the graph writes them back into the stores (`dp_setSave` → `gala.save`, etc.). So the save is rewritten on every action.\n\n**Start up:** `Logic/Start Up` reads `gala.state` (the persisted store), waits for the store to be ready, mends the save (`su_open` → `save.mend`), writes it back (`su_setSave`), sets `ready`. It also fetches the `GameChange` collection and applies the grown-ups' changes once (`su_apply` → `settings/apply.js` `applyOnce`), with a 4s timeout.\n\n**The backend today:** NodeGX cloud services (endpoint `http://127.0.0.1:8585`, appId `backend_mvi4i1s8eudt8`). `nodegx.security.json`:\n- `GameChange` collection: find/get public, create/update/delete nobody.\n- functions: `saveChange` and `removeChange` callable by `role:grownup`; `claimGrownup` by `authenticated`.\n- `signup: public`.\n- Cloud functions: `saveChange` (validates a grown-up's change against `checkChange`, writes to `GameChange`), `removeChange` (deletes a row), `claimGrownup` (makes the first signed-in account the `grownup` role).\n- Auth: `Pages/Grownups` has `net.noodl.user.User` (who is signed in: `authenticated`, `roles`, `username`), `SignUp`, `LogIn`, `LogOut`. Sign-in/sign-up is username + password. The first account to sign up claims the `grownup` role.\n\n**What the feature touches (named):**\n- `app/scripts/game/save.js` — the save's shape and repairs; the thing to store per child.\n- `app/components/Logic/Start Up` (`su_gala` GlobalStore, `su_open`, `su_setSave`, `su_storeReady`, `su_ready`) — where the save is opened and written at boot.\n- `app/components/Game/Dispatcher` (`dp_save`, `dp_setSave`, `dp_fn`) — where the save is written on every action.\n- `app/components/Pages/Grownups` (`gp_userNode`, `gp_login1`, `gp_signup1`, `gp_logout1`, `gp_claimFn`, `gp_view`) — the only sign-in UI today.\n- `app/components/App` (Router, start page) — where a sign-in page would sit.\n- `app/components/__cloud__/*` and `app/nodegx.security.json` — the backend pattern to follow.\n- `app/scripts/settings/apply.js` / `check.js` — the existing \"fetch from the backend at startup\" pattern.\n- `app/scripts/view/grownups.js` — the grown-ups' page view model.\n- `tools/sync-check.mjs`, `tools/check.src.js` — how the shared checker is kept in sync (a pattern to reuse for any new server-side validation).\n\nNow the plan.\n\n## The plan\n\n### Data model\n\nServer collections:\n1. `Player` — one row per child. Fields: `familyId` (the grown-up's user id), `name` (display name, e.g. \"Léa\"), `userId` (the child's account id), `createdAt`, `createdBy`. This is the family roster.\n2. `Progress` — one row per child. Fields: `userId`, `save` (the save JSON as a string), `rev` (integer, incremented on each accepted write), `updatedAt`, `deviceId` (last writer, for diagnostics). \n\nActually, do we need a separate `Player` collection if the child's account already has a username? We need the family link and the display name. The child's account username could be the display name, but usernames must be unique across the whole backend, so two families could collide on \"Lea\". So a `Player` row with a family-scoped display name is better. Also the grown-up needs to list their children.\n\nPermissions (`nodegx.security.json`):\n- `Player`: find/get `nobody` (only via cloud functions), create/update/delete `nobody`.\n- `Progress`: find/get `nobody`, create/update/delete `nobody`.\n- All access through cloud functions, following the existing `saveChange` pattern. This keeps the client from reading other families' data and from writing arbitrary saves.\n\nCloud functions (new, under `app/components/__cloud__/`):\n- `createChild` — callable by `role:grownup`. Input: `name`, `username`, `pin`. Creates a child account (or the grown-up creates it via the sign-up flow) and a `Player` row linking it to the caller's family. Returns the child's `userId` and display name.\n- `listChildren` — callable by `role:grownup`. Returns the caller's family roster (id, name, whether the child has a save).\n- `loadProgress` — callable by `authenticated`. Returns the caller's own `Progress` row (save + rev), or nothing. A child can only load their own.\n- `saveProgress` — callable by `authenticated`. Input: `save` (string), `baseRev`. Validates the save (shape, size, version), then writes with `rev = baseRev + 1` if the stored rev equals `baseRev`; otherwise returns the stored row so the client can reconcile. This is the optimistic-concurrency write.\n- `claimProgress` — callable by `authenticated`. Used on first sign-in: if the caller has no `Progress` row, create one from the local save. If one exists, return it (the client decides). This is the \"don't lose local progress\" step.\n- `deleteChild` / `resetProgress` — callable by `role:grownup` (optional, for the grown-up to remove a child or reset their progress).\n\nWait — how does the child get an account? NodeGX `SignUp` is public. Options:\n(a) The grown-up signs up the child from the grown-ups page: enters a display name, a username, and a PIN; the app calls `SignUp` with those credentials. But `SignUp` logs the grown-up out and logs the child in (it's a session-level sign-up). That would disrupt the grown-up's session. Hmm. Actually, does NodeGX have an admin \"create user\" cloud function? There's `noodl.cloud.addusertorole` (used in claimGrownup) but not a create-user. Let me think.\n\nActually, the cleanest approach given NodeGX's primitives: the child signs up themselves with a username + PIN, and the grown-up \"approves\" them into the family. But the requirement says the grown-up sets them up. \n\nAlternative: the grown-up creates the child's account by signing up on the child's behalf, then signs back in. That's clunky.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe the intended model is simpler: the family has one grown-up account. Children don't have their own accounts at all; instead, the grown-up creates \"profiles\" (Player rows) under the family, and a child signs in by picking their profile and entering a PIN. The PIN is checked by a cloud function against a hash stored on the Player row. This avoids NodeGX user accounts for children entirely, which fits \"they don't have an email address\" and \"a grown-up sets them up\" and \"two children share one device\".\n\nBut then \"sign in and find their game on another device\" — the child signs in with a family code + profile + PIN. The family code identifies the family (the grown-up's account). Hmm, but the child would need to know the family code. Alternatively, the child signs in with a globally-unique username + PIN, and the Player row maps username → family.\n\nLet me weigh:\n- **Model A: child = NodeGX user account.** Pros: uses the platform's auth, sessions, roles; `User` node gives `authenticated`/`username`; the backend can scope by `userId`. Cons: `SignUp` is public and session-level; creating an account for someone else is awkward; usernames are global.\n- **Model B: child = profile row + PIN, no account.** Pros: fits the family model exactly; the grown-up fully controls creation; no global username collisions (the family code + profile name is enough); children never see a real account. Cons: we implement PIN hashing and session ourselves; the child's \"session\" is a token we mint; we must be careful about security.\n\nGiven the requirements (children 8–13, no email, grown-up sets them up, two children share a device), Model B is a better fit and is what I'd propose. But it's more work and more security surface. Model A is less work and leans on the platform.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what's most defensible and least risky. The existing app already has a grown-up account with username/password and a `grownup` role. The natural extension: the grown-up creates child accounts. NodeGX's `SignUp` node — can it be called while signed in as the grown-up without losing the session? Probably it replaces the session. That's a real problem.\n\nActually, maybe there's a cloud-side way: a cloud function can create a user? The node types seen include `noodl.cloud.addusertorole`, `noodl.cloud.listusersinrole`, `noodl.cloud.getuserroles`, `noodl.cloud.request`, `noodl.cloud.response`. There might be a `noodl.cloud.createuser` or similar. I don't know. I should flag this as an uncertainty and propose a fallback.\n\nGiven the uncertainty, I'll propose Model B (family + profiles + PIN) as the primary design, because it doesn't depend on an unknown \"create user\" primitive and it matches the family requirement precisely. I'll note Model A as the alternative if NodeGX offers an admin create-user.\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider once more. With Model B, how does the child sign in on another device? They need to identify their family. Options:\n- The child enters a **family code** (a short code the grown-up shares, e.g. 6 characters) + their **profile name** + **PIN**. The cloud function looks up the family by code, finds the profile by name, checks the PIN, and returns a session token.\n- Or the child enters a **username** (globally unique, chosen by the grown-up) + PIN. The cloud function looks up the Player by username.\n\nThe username approach is simpler for the child (one field + PIN) and avoids a separate family code. The grown-up picks a username that's unique (the cloud function checks). I'll go with: **child signs in with a username and a PIN**, where the username is chosen by the grown-up and is unique across the backend. The Player row holds `username`, `pinHash`, `familyId`, `name`.\n\nSession: the cloud function returns a signed token (or we use NodeGX's session). Hmm, but if children aren't NodeGX users, we can't use NodeGX's session/roles. We'd mint our own token and store it in localStorage, and every cloud function call would carry it. That's a lot of custom auth.\n\nAlternatively — and this might be the sweet spot — **use NodeGX accounts for children too, but create them through a cloud function if one exists, else have the grown-up create them via a dedicated \"add a child\" flow that signs up and then signs the grown-up back in.** Ugh.\n\nLet me step back. The task is a *plan*, and the user explicitly said \"you decide what those are\" about the backend and server-side workflows. So I should make a clear decision, justify it, and flag the uncertainty. I'll pick the design that best fits the requirements and is implementable with the primitives I can see, and name the one thing I couldn't verify (whether NodeGX can create a user account server-side).\n\nDecision: **Children get their own NodeGX accounts** (username + PIN as the password), created by the grown-up. Rationale: it reuses the platform's auth, sessions, and roles; the `User` node already gives `authenticated`/`username`; the backend can scope progress by `userId`; and it avoids hand-rolling tokens and PIN hashing. The grown-up creates a child by entering a display name, a username, and a PIN; the app calls a new cloud function `createChild` which (if NodeGX allows server-side user creation) creates the account and the `Player` row. If NodeGX has no server-side user creation, the fallback is: the grown-up's page uses the `SignUp` node to create the child's account, then immediately signs the grown-up back in (the grown-up's credentials are re-entered once, or kept in memory for the moment). I'll flag this as the main unknown.\n\nHmm, but \"the grown-up's credentials re-entered\" is bad UX. Let me think if there's a better fallback. Actually, maybe the child signs up themselves on their own device with a username + PIN, and then the grown-up adds them to the family by entering the child's username in the grown-ups page (a `Player` row with `familyId` = grown-up's id, `userId` = the child's account id, looked up by username). That's clean: the child creates their own account (username + PIN, no email), and the grown-up links it to the family. But the requirement says \"a grown-up in the family should be the one who sets them up\" — the grown-up linking/approving is arguably \"setting them up\". And it avoids the create-user problem entirely. But it means the child has to sign up first, which is a bit backwards.\n\nI think the most robust plan is to present the account model with a clear primary path and a named fallback, and be explicit that the exact NodeGX user-creation primitive is the thing to verify first. That's honest and useful.\n\nLet me now also think about the **local-first / offline** design, which is the heart of the feature.\n\n### Local-first save with per-child namespacing\n\nToday: one persisted store `gala` with `storageKey: \"gala-game-v2\"`, holding `save`.\n\nChange: the save must be namespaced by who is signed in. Two children on one device each need their own save; a guest needs one too.\n\nProblem: the GlobalStore's `storageKey` is a build-time parameter, not a runtime input. So we can't just point the same store at a different key. Options:\n1. **Keep one store, but make the stored value a map of profiles → save.** i.e. `gala.save` becomes `{ profiles: { guest: {...}, \"user-123\": {...} }, active: \"guest\" }`. The dispatcher writes to `profiles[active]`. This keeps the existing store and storage key, and the \"save\" the rest of the app reads is `profiles[active]`. But that changes the shape everywhere the save is read (`gm_save`, `dp_save`, `su_open`, the stage's `sync`). Actually, we could keep the store's `save` key holding the *active* save (so nothing downstream changes), and add a second key `saves` holding the map. On switching profile, we write the active save into the map and load the new one into `save`. That's a small, contained change: a \"profile switch\" step in the graph.\n2. **Use a separate storage key per profile by writing to localStorage directly** from a script, bypassing the GlobalStore's persistence. But the GlobalStore is what persists; fighting it is messy.\n3. **One GlobalStore per profile** — not possible dynamically.\n\nOption 1 (a `saves` map keyed by profile id, with `save` holding the active one) is the cleanest. Let me detail it:\n- `gala` store keys: `save` (the active save, as today), `saves` (a map `{ [profileId]: save }`), `active` (the active profile id, e.g. `\"guest\"` or the child's user id).\n- On boot: read `saves` and `active`; if absent, migrate the existing `save` into `saves.guest` and set `active = \"guest\"`. This is the \"a child who already has progress on this device must not lose it\" guarantee for the *existing* single save: it becomes the guest profile's save.\n- On sign-in: if the child's profile has a local save in `saves`, use it; else if the server has one, load it; else adopt the guest save (first sign-in) — see below.\n- On sign-out: write the active save back into `saves[active]`, set `active = \"guest\"`, load `saves.guest` into `save`.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: the requirement \"a child who already has progress on this device must not lose it when they first sign in\". This means: a child plays as guest, then signs in for the first time. Their guest save should become their account's save. So on first sign-in, if the account has no server save, we take the guest save (or the local save under their profile, if any) and upload it. If the account already has a server save (they played on another device), we must not silently overwrite either. Policy: if the account has a server save and the guest save is non-trivial, we keep the server save as the account's save and preserve the guest save under the guest profile (so nothing is lost — the child can still play as guest, or the grown-up can choose). Actually, better: on first sign-in, if the account has no save, adopt the guest save. If it has one, ask (a simple choice: \"Continue where you left off on this device\" vs \"Load my saved game\") — but for 8–13 year olds, a one-tap choice with two clear buttons is fine, and the grown-up can help. Or default to the server save and keep the local one as a \"recovered\" copy. I'll propose: adopt the guest save when the account is empty; when both exist, show a one-tap choice, defaulting to the newer by `updatedAt`, and keep the other as a recoverable copy.\n\nWait, actually, re-reading: \"a child who already has progress on this device must not lose it when they first sign in.\" The simplest reading: the local progress must survive the sign-in. If the account is new (first sign-in ever), the local progress becomes the account's progress. If the account already has progress (they signed in before, or on another device), then... the local progress is presumably the same account's progress from before, or a guest's. The safe rule: never delete a local save on sign-in; always keep it under its profile; and when the account is empty, adopt it. I'll go with that.\n\n### Sync\n\n- Every action already rewrites the save. Add a **debounced push**: after a save change, if signed in and online, call `saveProgress` with the save and the last known `rev`. Debounce ~2–5s (and also push on `visibilitychange`/`pagehide`).\n- Keep a **durable outbox** in localStorage: `{ profileId, save, baseRev, dirty: true }`. Actually, since the save is a single blob and the store already persists it, the \"outbox\" is just \"the local save is newer than the last acked rev\". So store `lastAckedRev` per profile; if `localRev > lastAckedRev`, there's something to push. On reconnect (online event, or next action, or a timer), push.\n- **Offline:** the game is entirely client-side (rules, 3D, music), so it keeps working. The only network at boot is the `GameChange` fetch, which already has a 4s timeout and falls back. So offline play is already fine; we just must not block on the network at boot when signed in. So: load the local save first and start the game; sync in the background. Never gate `ready` on the network.\n- **Conflict:** `saveProgress` uses optimistic concurrency: it writes only if the stored `rev` equals the client's `baseRev`; otherwise it returns the stored row. On conflict, the client keeps the server's save as the account's save and preserves the local one as a recoverable copy (or, if the local save is strictly newer by `updatedAt` and the server's rev is unchanged since the client's last load, it can retry with the new baseRev). For a kids' game with sequential device use, conflicts should be rare; the important thing is that nothing is silently lost.\n\nHmm, \"nothing the child did should be lost\" if the connection drops mid-game. Since the save is written to localStorage on every action, and the outbox is the local save itself, nothing is lost locally. The only loss risk is a conflict overwriting a newer server save with an older local one, or vice versa. The optimistic-concurrency + keep-both policy handles it.\n\n### Sign-in UI\n\n- A new page `Pages/SignIn` (or a panel on the game page) reachable from the top bar. For children: a username field and a PIN field, a big \"Play\" button. For the grown-up: the existing `Pages/Grownups` sign-in, plus a \"My children\" section to add/remove children and reset their progress.\n- The start page is currently `/Pages/Game`. The requirement \"the game must still work for someone who never signs in\" means the start page stays the game, and sign-in is optional (a button in the top bar). Good — no forced sign-in.\n- The grown-up's page already exists; add a \"children\" section there.\n\n### What could go wrong\n\n- The GlobalStore `storageKey` can't be changed at runtime → solved by the `saves` map.\n- The `save` is read in many places; changing its shape would ripple. Keep `save` as the active save; add `saves`/`active` alongside.\n- The stage's `sync` and the dispatcher read `save` directly; they keep working because `save` is still the active save.\n- Boot ordering: the Start Up currently mends the save and sets `ready`. Adding a profile load must happen before `ready`, but must not wait on the network. So: load local profile synchronously (from the store), set `ready`, then sync in the background.\n- The `GameChange` fetch and the progress fetch both hit the backend at boot; keep them independent and both non-blocking.\n- Two children on one device: switching profiles must flush the current save into `saves` before loading the other. A missed flush loses the last few actions. Mitigate by flushing on every save change (the dispatcher already writes the active save; also write it into `saves[active]`), so `saves` is always current.\n- Actually, simpler: make `saves[active]` the source of truth and have the dispatcher write there, with `save` as a derived convenience. But that's a bigger change. Let me propose: the dispatcher writes the active save to `save` (as today) and a small \"profile\" script mirrors it into `saves[active]` on every change. Or: change `dp_setSave` to write to `saves` and have a subscription derive `save`. Hmm. I'll propose the mirror approach as the minimal change, and note the alternative.\n- Security: a child must not be able to read/write another child's progress. All progress access goes through cloud functions that use the caller's `userId`; the collections are `nobody` for direct access. The save is validated server-side (shape, size, version) so a tampered client can't store arbitrary data. Note: a child could still tamper with their own save's contents (e.g., stars) before uploading; for a kids' game that's low-stakes, but if it matters, the server would need to validate the save's semantics (hard, since the save is the whole game state). I'll flag this.\n- The grown-up's `GameChange` is family-wide (shared by all children of the family) — that's the existing behaviour and should stay (the school's rules are the family's). But note: if two families share a backend, `GameChange` is currently global (not scoped by family). That's an existing issue; the plan should scope `GameChange` by family too, or note it. Actually, `GameChange` is read publicly and applied by everyone — so all families on one backend share the same grown-up changes. That's a pre-existing design assumption (one backend per family/school). I'll note it.\n- Offline first sign-in: a child signs in on a device with no connection → can't authenticate. The game must still work as guest. So sign-in failure must fall back to guest, not block.\n- The PIN: children 8–13; a 4-digit PIN is guessable. Rate-limit sign-in attempts server-side. Also, the grown-up should be able to reset a child's PIN.\n- Data size: the save is a JSON blob; it could grow with the notebook (one entry per gala per season). Cap it and validate size server-side.\n- Version skew: the save has `v: 2` and `mend` handles older saves. A save from a newer app version on another device could be unreadable; `mend` starts fresh on unreadable input, which would *lose* progress. So the sync must not overwrite a newer-version save with a fresh one. Add a version check: if the server save's `v` is newer than the app's, don't overwrite; warn.\n- The `seed` is per-save and drives the simulation; two devices with the same save produce the same evening. Fine.\n- Clock: `updatedAt` from the client is untrusted; use the server's clock for `updatedAt` and `rev` for ordering.\n\n### Order of work\n\n1. **Verify the platform primitives** (the one unknown): can a cloud function create a user account? Can a cloud function read the caller's `userId`? (Yes — `noodl.cloud.request` gives `userId`.) Can the client call a cloud function with parameters and get a response? (Yes — `CloudFunction2`.) Decide the account model based on this.\n2. **Backend first**: add the `Player` and `Progress` collections, the security rules, and the cloud functions (`createChild`, `listChildren`, `loadProgress`, `saveProgress`, `claimProgress`). Deploy them (`deploy_cloud_functions`). Test each with a direct call.\n3. **Local profile store**: change `Logic/Start Up` and `Game/Dispatcher` to keep a `saves` map and an `active` profile; migrate the existing `save` into `saves.guest`. Verify the game still plays exactly as before for a guest.\n4. **Sign-in UI**: add the child sign-in (username + PIN) and the grown-up's \"my children\" section. Wire `LogIn`/`LogOut` (or the custom session) and the profile switch.\n5. **Sync**: add the debounced push, the outbox/`lastAckedRev`, the online/offline handling, and the conflict policy. Verify offline play and reconnect.\n6. **First sign-in adoption**: adopt the guest save when the account is empty; keep both when it isn't.\n7. **Tests and checks**: extend `gala-rules` tests for the save's shape; add a `tools/check-*.mjs` for the new server-side validation; drive the app with `drive_page`.\n\n### What I'd check to know it works\n\n- Guest play is unchanged: play a full gala as guest, reload, progress is there.\n- Sign in on device A, play, sign out; sign in on device B, the progress is there.\n- Two children on one device: child 1 plays, signs out; child 2 signs in, sees their own (different) progress; child 1 signs back in, sees theirs.\n- First sign-in with existing local progress: play as guest, sign in to a fresh account, the guest progress is now the account's.\n- Offline: sign in, go offline, play, reload (still offline), progress is there; go online, the progress reaches the server.\n- Connection drops mid-game: kill the network during a run, finish the run, reload; nothing lost.\n- Conflict: play on A offline, play on B, then bring A online; neither save is silently lost.\n- Security: a child cannot read another child's progress (call `loadProgress` as another user); a child cannot write a `Player` row; a non-grown-up cannot call `createChild`.\n- The existing grown-ups' flow still works (saveChange/removeChange/claimGrownup).\n- `node --test gala-rules/tests/*.test.mjs` passes; `node tools/check-rules.mjs`, `node tools/check-views.mjs` pass.\n- `validate_project` reports 0 errors; `drive_page` from the start page.\n\n### How I found my way around\n\nEasy: the folder is small and well-commented; the scripts are pure and readable; the `tools/` sync scripts explain the generated files; the cloud functions are small and self-describing; the security file names the roles and permissions.\n\nHard: the `reference/src` the tools copy from is gone, so the stage scripts are generated from a source that isn't here; the NodeGX node semantics (`net.noodl.user.*`, `GlobalStore`, `CloudFunction2`) aren't documented in the folder, so I inferred them from usage; the `save` shape is spread across `save.js`, `plan.js`, `data.js`, and `actions.js`; the `ui` store's shape is in the Start Up node's parameters; the app's boot order is only visible by reading the Start Up connections.\n\nUnsure: whether NodeGX can create a user account server-side (the account model hinges on this); whether the GlobalStore's `storageKey` can be changed at runtime (I assumed not); whether `net.noodl.user.LogOut`'s input is really `login` (the connection says so, but it's odd); whether the backend is one-per-family or shared (the `GameChange` collection is global); the exact `net.noodl.user.User` outputs beyond `authenticated`/`roles`/`username`.\n\nNow, let me also double check: is there a `nodegx.routes.json` or `nodegx.styles.json`? The skill mentions them but the find didn't show them. The Router node holds the pages inline (`pages: { startPage, routes }`). So routes are in the App component, not a separate file. Good to note.\n\nLet me verify there's no nodegx.routes.json / nodegx.styles.json.\n\nAlso, let me check the `Grownups/Words` component (it appeared in the auth grep) and the `Grownups/Row Editor`. And let me confirm the `net.noodl.user.User` node's ports by looking at the nodes.json ports array if present.\n\nLet me do a couple more targeted reads.", "summary": []}
